t.card(p,'02 03 04 05','Invented Namibian teaching case: a firm pays a US$50 supplier invoice from a transferable bank balance. Its quotation changes from US$1 = N$18 to US$1 = N$20, with fees ignored and the US-dollar invoice unchanged. The N$/rand peg remains one-to-one. The firm also seeks expansion finance, but repayment capacity is uncertain; potential share investors have not committed. Describe three monetary/exchange features in the case (6). Explain the exchange-rate change’s effect on the invoice and possible export competitiveness, with response conditions (7). Discuss the distinct contributions and limits of commercial banks, the central bank and a stock exchange for the firm’s financial needs (7).','A transferable bank balance supports payment; money serves as a medium of exchange; the quotation states domestic currency per US dollar. The invoice rises from N$900 to N$1,000 because each US dollar costs more domestic currency. An exporter might become more competitive in foreign prices, but gains require overseas response and supply capacity and can be offset by imported inputs. A commercial bank can provide payments and assess credit, but uncertain repayment limits loan suitability. Central-bank stability supports the environment without approving individual loans. A stock exchange can support equity trading and suitable new issues, but resale alone is not fresh issuer finance and investors are not committed. Match the instrument to the need and credible terms.',['Context/data description (6): transferable balance (1), not a physical card as money (1); medium of exchange (1), supplier payment settles transaction (1); N$ per US$ quotation (1), prices one currency in another (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): invoice originally N$900 (1), later N$1,000 (1), each dollar dearer (1); possible lower foreign export price (1), customer response needed (1), supply capacity needed (1), imported input costs can offset benefit (1). Both sides/judgement (7): commercial-bank payment/credit role (1), repayment risk limits credit (1); central-bank system stability (1), not direct individual loan approval (1); exchange/new equity can fit expansion (1), resale/investor interest do not ensure fresh funds (1); judge by service fit and credible terms (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][2]
t.card(p,'05','Evaluate financial-institution contributions in two seven-mark plans. Invented Case 1: a Namibian household needs to receive wages and make regular payments, values access and faces service charges. Case 2: an expanding company needs long-term ownership finance, can share control but must attract investors and meet governance responsibilities. In both cases distinguish commercial-bank services, central-bank stability and a stock exchange; the Namibia-dollar/rand peg is maintained.','Case 1: a suitable commercial-bank account can provide the required receiving and payment services, but charges/access conditions matter. Central-bank stability supports the environment; a share issue is not a substitute for transaction services. Case 2: suitable new equity can finance expansion and an organised exchange can support investor participation, but ownership and governance costs matter and resale does not itself give new funds to the company. A commercial loan creates repayment obligations and central-bank policy does not guarantee investor funds. Choose the service matching the actual need under feasible terms.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for receiving wages (1), making payments (1); argument against charges (1), possible access limits (1); condition suitable reliable service terms (1); judgement favours matching bank services (1), distinguishes system stability/securities trading from household payment need (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for new equity finances expansion (1), organised resale market can support participation (1); argument against shared ownership (1), governance cost/uncertain demand (1); condition credible issue and capacity (1); judgement conditionally favours equity (1), distinguishes new issue from resale and avoids assuming central-bank finance (1). Total 14.'])
t.finish()
